  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 256 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough over the Four-Corners Region will finally \neject northeastward Wednesday night into Thursday, resulting in a \nlift back north of the front through the CWA as a warm front in \nresponse to surface cyclogenesis in the Southern/Central Plains. As \nlow to mid-level isentropic ascent/WAA increases in this process, \nanother round of showers and thunderstorms is forecast late \nWednesday night into Thursday morning with HREF and REFS membership \nmost in alignment with most widespread coverage across \ncentral/northeastern MO into west-central IL. Since PW will again be \nanomalously high, there will at least be a low threat of locally \nheavy rainfall, but again the overall threat will again be limited by \nmainly progressive thunderstorm motions. There has been a trend in \nmodel guidance toward a more constructive interaction of the trough \nwith a potent northern stream trough, resulting in solutions with \nstronger large-scale ascent and a deeper and slightly more mature \nsurface cyclone passing just to the west and northwest of the CWA \nThursday afternoon and evening. This evolution would result in \ngreater development of showers and thunderstorms along a trailing \ncold front and perhaps a more favorable setup, conceptually \nspeaking, for severe weather. That being said, there are still \nsolutions with weaker ascent and moreso a frontal wave with isolated \nshowers, maybe a thunderstorm or two. These factors aside, the \nimpact of early day clouds and precip will heavily influence how \nmuch instability is available with uncertainty reflected in the \ninterquartile range of SBCAPE in the REFS spanning 250-500 J/kg to \n1000+ J/kg.\n\nBehind the cold front passage Thursday night, strong and persistent \nCAA will be present on Friday leading to much cooler, more \nseasonable airmass being established across the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley through Saturday night with high temperatures in the \nmid-50s to mid-60s F and low temperatures in the mid-30s to mid-40s \nF. Exactly how much temperatures cool by Saturday morning and Sunday \nmorning will be determined by whether or not a surface anticyclone \nis favorable positioned across the region during nocturnal \nradiational cooling. The NBM has 20 to 50 percent probabilities of \nsufficiently cool temperatures for frost formation (~36 F or less) \nin rural and/or low-lying locations both Saturday and Sunday \nmornings, but there is some spread in the temperature distribution.\n\nGlobal model guidance is in agreement a warming trend will begin \nSunday as the surface anticyclone shifts to the east and low-level \nsoutherly flow/WAA ensues beneath upper-level southwesterly flow \nSunday into early next week. In fact, the entire NBM temperature \ndistribution is back above average by Monday afternoon, although \nnowhere near the magnitude of current above average temperatures. \nThere is disagreement, however, in if an upper-level trough passes \naround Monday, providing the next opportunity for precipitation.",
